#### 1.2.2 \(\mathsf{J/\psi}\) polarization

To extract the \(\mathsf{J/\psi}\) polarization parameters from data, we need to define the polarization reference frame first. As shown in Figure 5, \(\mathsf{p_{1}}\) and \(\mathsf{p_{2}}\) correspond to the colliding proton's momentum in the \(\mathsf{J/\psi}\) rest frame, which defines the production plane. Y axis is perpendicular to the production plane. The difference between Helicity frame and Collins-Soper frame is that the Z axis in Helicity frame is defined along the sum of \(\mathsf{p_{1}}\) and \(\mathsf{p_{2}}\) vector, while the Z axis in Collins-Soper frame is defined as the bisector of \(\mathsf{p_{1}}\) and \(\mathsf{p_{2}}\) vector. Consequently, after Y axis and Z axis is aligned, the X axis is determined. In the present study, we usually measure the polarization parameters in Helicity frame and Collins-Soper frame.

To remove the effect of the arbitrary choice of reference frame, \(\lambda_{\mathsf{inv}}\) is the specific quantity remaining the same in both the Helicity frame and the Collins-Soper frame.

\[\lambda_{\mathsf{inv}}=\frac{\lambda_{\emptyset}+3\lambda_{\Phi}}{1-\lambda_{ \Phi}} \tag{1.1}\]A particle may be observed preferentially in a state belonging to a definite subset of the possible eigenstates of the angular momentum component \(\mathsf{J}_{z}\) along a characteristic quantization axis. When this happens, the particle is said polarized [35]. Polarization of \(\mathsf{J}/\uppsi\) indicates the anisotropic spatial distribution of lepton decay from \(\mathsf{J}/\uppsi\) with respect to \(\mathsf{J}/\uppsi\) momentum. And the differential cross section of \(\mathsf{J}/\uppsi\) decay products can be written as the Fourier series expansion as following:

\[\frac{\upomega^{2}\mathsf{N}}{\upomega\mathsf{cos}\upomega\upphi}\propto \mathsf{I}+\lambda_{\upomega}\mathsf{cos}^{2}\uptheta+\lambda_{\upphi} \mathsf{sin}^{2}\uptheta\mathsf{cos}(2\upphi)+\lambda_{\upphi\upphi}\mathsf{ sin}(2\uptheta)\mathsf{cos}\upphi, \tag{1.2}\]

Figure 5: Definition of the polarization frames: helicity(HX) and Collins-Soper(CS) frames [34]. The y-axis is perpendicular to the production plane, which is defined by the momenta of the two colliding protons, represented by \(\mathsf{p}_{1}\) and \(\mathsf{p}_{2}\) respectively.

where the coefficients \(\lambda_{\theta}\), \(\lambda_{\phi}\) and \(\lambda_{\theta\phi}\) are parameters determining the probability density distribution of the decay products with respect to the polarization (\(\hat{\mathpzc{z}}\)) axis in the J/\(\psi\) rest frame. As illustrated by Figure 6, when \(\lambda_{\theta}=+1\) the positron prefers moving parallelly to z axis and when \(\lambda_{\theta}=-1\) the positron prefers moving orthogonally to z axis.

Figure 6: The extreme cases that when polarization parameter \(\lambda_{\theta}\) is equal to +1 and -1 respectively. The probability that positron decay from the direction is equal to the distance of the corresponding surface from the origin [35].

The integration over \(\cos\!\theta\) or \(\phi\) yields the one-dimensional distribution of \(\phi\) and \(\cos\!\theta\) respectively,

\[W(\cos\!\theta)\propto\frac{1}{3+\lambda_{\theta}}(1+\lambda_{\theta}\cos^{2} \!\theta), \tag{1.3}\]

\[W(\phi)\propto 1+\frac{2\lambda_{\phi}}{3+\lambda_{\theta}}\mbox{$\cos 2\phi$} \tag{1.4}\]

Due to the nature of differential cross section of positron decay from \(J/\psi\) is probability which should be greater than or equal to 0 for every direction. This requirement exerts constraints on the \(J/\psi\) polarization parameters so that the admissible set of \(J/\psi\) polarization parameters is shown in Figure 7.

