## Chapter Systematic Uncertainty

In experimental measurements, results of the measurements can be affected by many factors introduced by choice, configuration, threshold and the others. It is important to know their effects along with the measurement results, which also suggests the level of accuracy of measurement results. This chapter will introduce main factors that may affect the measurement results and how they are calculated.

### 5.1 \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) Weight function

The choice of \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) weight function (see Eq. 3.2), which was obtained by fitting the measured \(\mathrm{J/\uppsi}\)\(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) spectrum, could to some extent affect the \(\mathrm{J/\uppsi}\) polarization parameter estimation. To calculate the systematic uncertainty from the \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) weight function, another function \(\mathrm{PowLaw(p_{T})}\) (see Figure 36) is utilized to recalculate the \(\mathrm{J/\uppsi}\) reconstruction efficiency with the following parameters.

\[\mathrm{PowLaw(p_{T})}=A(1+(\frac{\mathrm{x}}{\mathrm{B}})^{2})^{\mathrm{C}} \tag{5.1}\]

where, A = 4.90151, B = 3.30694 and C = -4.86445.The variation of polarization parameters is taken as a systematic uncertainty from the \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) weight function.

### \(\rm p_{T}\) smearing

Compared with the invariant mass distribution of \(\rm J/\psi\) from the experimental data, the distribution obtained from embedding is narrower. In order to reproduce the invariant mass distribution in embedding to better account for the momentum resolution, the transverse mo

Figure 36: The comparison of Levy function and Powerlaw function fit to the measured \(\rm J/\psi\) production cross section [62].

mentum is smeared by adding a component from Double Crystal Ball (DCB) function, which reads

\[P(p_{T}^{rec},p_{T}^{MC})\propto\begin{cases}A\times(B-R)^{-n},R<-\alpha\\ e^{-\frac{R^{2}}{2}},-\alpha<R<\beta\\ C\times(D+R)^{-m},R>\beta\end{cases}\]

where

\[A=(\frac{n}{|\alpha|})^{n}*\exp(-\frac{\alpha^{2}}{2}),\]

\[B=\frac{n}{|\alpha|}-|\alpha|,\]

\[C=(\frac{m}{|\beta|})^{m}\times e^{-\frac{\beta^{2}}{2}}\]

\[D=\frac{m}{|\beta|}-|\beta|\]

\[R=(\frac{p_{T}^{rec}-p_{T}^{MC}}{p_{T}^{MC}}-\mu)/\frac{\sigma_{p_{T}}}{p_{T}}\]

The parameters are fit by \(\chi^{2}\) minimization.

Considering the resolution of \(p_{T}\) measurement, \(p_{T}\) is smeared using the function \(fReso(p_{Tmc})\).

\[fReso(p_{Tmc})=\sqrt{a^{2}x^{2}+b^{2}} \tag{5.2}\]

where, a = 0.0035324 and b = 0.00829197The resolution is parameterized by a Double Crystal Ball function and a \(\tt p_{T}\) dependent resolution function. In this systematic uncertainty calculation, the parameter a is adjusted to a = 0.0035324 + 0.0003 and a = 0.0035324 - 0.0003.

### DCA cut efficiency

Due to the short lifetime of \(\mathrm{J/\psi}\) meson [63], to reconstruct the event of \(\mathrm{J/\psi}\) decay to dielectron, it is required the distance of closest approach \(\mathrm{DCA}<\tt lcm\) by default. The distance between the electron track is within 1cm away from vertex.

To calculate the systematic uncertainty from DCA configuration, DCA cut is adjusted from \(\mathrm{DCA}<\tt lcm\) to \(\mathrm{DCA}<\tt 0.8cm\) and \(\mathrm{DCA}<\tt 1.2cm\) and the maximum variation of polarization parameters' value are assigned as systematic uncertainty from DCA cut configuration.

### nHitsFit cut efficiency

It has been introduced that the charged particle's trajectory is reconstructed from the readout of TPC by lining up the hits position left by the charged particle. The quantity nHitsFit refers to how many hits are used to reconstructed a track. So the nHitsFit cut affects whether a track is defined as a good track and then make a difference in \(\mathrm{J/\psi}\) reconstruction. To calculate the systematic uncertainty from this aspect, the nHitsFit cut is adjusted to \(\tt nHitsFit\geq\tt 18\), \(\tt nHitsFit\geq\tt 19\), \(\tt nHitsFit\geq\tt 22\) and \(\tt nHitsFit\geq\tt 25\) and the maximum variation introduced by these changes is taken as systematic uncertainty from nHitsFit cut.

